+# Daily Grind
+
+Requires Node.js 22 or newer. No dependencies or asset compilation are required.
+
+- `npm start` runs the Node server on `PORT` (default: `11222`).
+- `npm run build` checks JavaScript syntax without starting or importing the server.
+
+The homepage posts newsletter email addresses to `POST /api/newsletter`.
+Validated signups are appended to `.data/newsletter.jsonl` on this server.
+Set `NEWSLETTER_FILE` to choose another private file on persistent storage.
+The server uses an explicit public-file allowlist, so signup data and source
+files are never served. Storage failures return an error rather than a false
+success. This collects subscriptions; it does not send newsletter campaigns.
+
+The form shows progress, success, and a retry message for failed, blocked,
+or timed-out requests. It also supports native form submission without
+JavaScript.
+
+All responses carry the enforced header:
+
+```http
+Connection-Allowlist: (response-origin "https://images.unsplash.com")
+```
+
+Only this origin and the exact HTTPS Unsplash photo host are permitted.
+Redirects and WebRTC remain blocked by default. Headers are applied uniformly,
+including errors and script responses, without varying by request destination.
+CSP additionally permits connections and form submissions only to this origin,
+and permits Unsplash only for images. Scripts require a fresh response nonce;
+styles are local, with no inline styles or event handlers.
+
+Deploy behind HTTPS. Connection-Allowlist requires browser support and a secure
+context; unsupported browsers receive CSP restrictions, but CSP cannot cover
+every network channel (such as WebRTC and top-level script navigation).
+See the [Chrome connection allowlist guide](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/modern-web-guidance/guides/security/restrict-outbound-connections)
+for the browser support and fallback limits. The allowlisted photo host is
+itself a trusted destination, so the policy is not a guarantee against all
+possible disclosure to an allowed origin.
